<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Ober Ost</h1><p class="st-meta">1915 CE – 1918 CE · capital Kovno</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">German military state in occupied Lithuania, Courland and western Belarus during the First World War, run by Hindenburg and Ludendorff's staff.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the German offensives of 1915 drove Russian armies out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the Grodno-Białystok region, the territory was placed under the Supreme Commander in the East, Oberbefehlshaber Ost, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a> as chief of staff. The headquarters moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kovno</a> (Kaunas).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ober Ost was a military administration that tried to remake the land according to German plans: it requisitioned food, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and livestock, imposed forced labour and controlled movement through passes. It also sponsored newspapers, schools and surveys among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a>, seeing the region as a German colony.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> in March 1918, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> allowed client institutions, including the Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Taryba</a>, which had declared independence in February 1918, and planned a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia-1918">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a> (1918). Germany's defeat in November 1918 ended the administration, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia</a> emerged.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ober-ost.jpg" alt="Ober Ost" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Jansz van Santen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B10_029-LIVONIA_Vulgo_Lyetland.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1915 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kovno</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Paul von Hindenburg, Erich Ludendorff</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran and Catholic Christianity, Judaism, Orthodoxy</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Lithuanian, Latvian, Polish, Yiddish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oberbefehlshaber Ost administration, Land Ober Ost</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1916&amp;focus=ober-ost" aria-label="Ober Ost on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5153 374 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ober Ost lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5473 630L5300 622M5473 630L5556 480"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5556 480h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5473 630h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1916&amp;focus=ober-ost">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

